---
functor: 1
title: frac, sqrt, text, join, stack
---

set heading(numbering: none)

A call is a name and arguments in round brackets, with commas between them. Structure calls such as `mat(...)` are on [Grids, matrices, vectors, cases](structures/grids.html) and [Fences and wrap](structures/fences.html).

| Call | Arguments | Result |
|:--|:--|:-:|
| `frac(a, b)` | top, bottom | $frac(a, b)$ |
| `sqrt(x)` | one | $sqrt(x^2 + y^2)$ |
| `binom(n, k)` | top, bottom | $binom(n, k)$ |
| `join(o.up, r.up)` | two single symbols | $join(o.up, r.up)$ |
| `stack(lt, sim)` | top, bottom | $stack(lt, sim)$ |
| `text(x)` | one | $text(x)$ |

# frac

`frac(a + b, 2)`. In display math the parts are full size. In text they are smaller.

# sqrt

`sqrt(x)` draws the root sign over its argument. The sign grows with the content. The n-th root has no form yet.

# join

`join(a, b)` sets two symbols so that they touch and become one glyph. Build each side first: `join(circ, r.up)`. The logo of Functor uses `join(o.up, r.up.arrow)`.

# stack

`stack(a, b)` sets `a` over `b` and makes one glyph. It is the vertical partner of `join`. When the font has the result, Functor uses the font glyph: `stack(lt, sim)` gives ≲, `stack(prec, eq)` gives ⪯ and `stack(to, to.flip)` gives ⇄. Text in quotes is set smaller, so `stack("def", eq)` puts "def" over the equals sign.

The glyph is a relation when one of its parts is a relation.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `stack(lt, eq)`, `stack(gt, eq)` | $stack(lt, eq), stack(gt, eq)$ |
| `stack(lt, sim)`, `stack(gt, sim)` | $stack(lt, sim), stack(gt, sim)$ |
| `stack(lt, gt)`, `stack(gt, lt)` | $stack(lt, gt), stack(gt, lt)$ |
| `stack(prec, eq)`, `stack(succ, eq)`, `stack(prec, sim)` | $stack(prec, eq), stack(succ, eq), stack(prec, sim)$ |
| `stack(approx, eq)`, `stack(triangle, eq)` | $stack(approx, eq), stack(triangle, eq)$ |
| `stack(subset, eq.not)`, `stack(supset, eq.not)` | $stack(subset, eq.not), stack(supset, eq.not)$ |
| `stack(to, to)`, `stack(to.flip, to.flip)`, `stack(to, to.flip)` | $stack(to, to), stack(to.flip, to.flip), stack(to, to.flip)$ |
| `stack("def", eq)` | $stack("def", eq)$ |

# text

Today `text(x)` sets its argument as math, the same as `x`. For upright words, use quotes: `"if"`. See [Common errors](help/errors.html).

# Wrong number of arguments

A call with the wrong number of arguments is an error, and the hint shows the correct form, for example "Write `frac(top, bottom)`."
